    "answer": {
      "short": "All three functions are $\\{(0,0)\\}$.\n\nEach has domain $\\{0\\}$ and sends $0$ to $0$.",
      "worked": "For real $x$ and $y$, both $x^2$ and $y^2$ are nonnegative. Therefore $x^2+y^2=0$ holds only when $x=y=0$, so $f=\\{(0,0)\\}$.\n\nFor $g$, both square roots are real only when $x\\ge0$ and $-x\\ge0$. Hence $x=0$, and $g(0)=\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0}=0$. Thus $g=\\{(0,0)\\}$.\n\nSince $h$ has domain $\\{0\\}$ and range $\\{0\\}$, it must send $0$ to $0$. Thus $h=\\{(0,0)\\}$.\n\nTherefore $f=g=h=\\{(0,0)\\}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
